Welcome to Term49X!

Term49X is a BlackBerry 10 terminal emulator for the
BlackBerry Classic (Q20) hardware — physical keyboard,
trackpad, and belt keys — and for other BB10 devices. It
continues Term48/Term49/Term49X, and adds multiple shells
(tabs + session switcher), scrollback search, and
tap-to-open URLs.

This file is linked into your home directory on first
launch. Topics:

1. QUICK START
2. SESSIONS (TABS), SWITCHER AND SEARCH
3. PINYIN / 双拼 INPUT BAR (小鹤双拼)
4. CLASSIC HARDWARE (trackpad, belt keys)
5. MOUSE, SCROLLBACK, COPY/PASTE
6. SHIFT+TAB AND KEY TABLES
7. PREFERENCES
8. WHAT'S INSTALLED
9. SOURCE / HELP

===========================================================

1. QUICK START

Special keys (Ctrl, Alt, arrows, etc.) via metamode:
touch the top-left corner of the screen (just below the
tab bar), then press a key. A green 'M' appears top-right while metamode is on.
Touch the corner again to leave metamode.

Default metamode keys:

  h: left arrow      j: down arrow
  k: up arrow        l: right arrow
  t: tab             e: escape
  c: control         a: alt
  v: paste           s: rescreen

Arrow keys stay in metamode so you can move repeatedly.
Other keys exit metamode after one use.

Example: Ctrl-C — touch top-left (metamode), 'c' (ctrl),
then 'c' again.

On a physical keyboard you can also:
  - hold Space to toggle metamode (reliable on Classic)
  - hold letter keys for uppercase (keyhold_actions)

Double-tap Shift is optional and device-dependent. The default
is double-tap *right* Shift (metamode_doubletap_key =
KEYCODE_RIGHT_SHIFT). On the BlackBerry Classic (Q20) that
does **not** work: the hardware does not deliver a distinct
right-Shift event the way Term49X expects. Use hold-Space or
the top-left hitbox on Classic instead. On other devices (or
Bluetooth keyboards that send right Shift) double-tap may work;
you can also set metamode_doubletap_key to left Shift if your
keyboard only reports that key.

On the Classic, prefer the trackpad for arrows and the
Menu belt key for persistent Alt (see below).

===========================================================

2. SESSIONS (TABS), SWITCHER AND SEARCH

The terminal runs several shells at once. Each one has its
own screen, scrollback, parser state and child process.

TAB BAR (top of the screen, one tab per shell)

  +        open a new shell (a new tab)
  =        open the session switcher overlay (hamburger)
  tab      tap to switch to that shell
  swipe    swipe left/right across the bar to go to the
           next / previous shell
  dot      a small dot on a tab means that shell produced
           output while you were looking at another one
  active   tapping the active tab opens the switcher

Hide the bar to get the whole screen back: metamode '0'.
The terminal grid is re-flowed to use the space.

SWITCHER OVERLAY ('=' on the bar, or metamode 'b')

  - one row per shell: tap the row to switch to it
  - R: rename that shell
  - x: close that shell (its process gets SIGHUP)
  - "+ New session" at the bottom
  - x (top right) closes the overlay

KEYBOARD (all inside metamode, after the corner / Space)

  n   new session            b   session switcher
  r   rename current         x   close current
  f   search scrollback      [ ] previous / next session
  1-9 switch to session 1..9    0   show/hide the tab bar

These bindings are built in, so they work with an old
~/.term49rc that has no entries for them; your own
metamode bindings are checked first and still win.

To bind them yourself, add to metamode_func_keys in
~/.term49rc:

  ("s", "session_menu")   ("n", "session_new")
  ("r", "session_rename") ("f", "session_search")
  ("[", "session_prev")   ("]", "session_next")
  ("x", "session_close")  ("0", "session_bar")

SEARCH (metamode 'f')

  Type to search the whole scrollback (case-insensitive).
    Enter / Tab / >      next hit
    < / Left / Up        previous hit
    x / Escape           close and return to the live view
  Hits are highlighted; the current one is underlined and
  scrolled into the middle of the screen.

URLS

  A short tap on a http://, https://, ftp://, www. or
  mailto: token opens it in the browser. Taps that scrolled
  the view or that are part of a text selection are ignored.

NOTES

  - Output from a shell you are not looking at waits in the
    tty buffer and is read the moment you switch to that
    tab. Nothing is lost, but a program that writes
    non-stop (yes, a fast log) blocks until you look at it.
  - A shell that exits stays as a red tab so its last output
    stays readable. If it was the only session, the app
    exits, as it always has.
  - Session names are not saved across app restarts.
  - Up to 8 sessions; each one costs a scrollback buffer.

===========================================================

3. PINYIN / 双拼 INPUT BAR (小鹤双拼)

The terminal has its own pinyin input method (no system
IME needed). It is on by default; the bottom row shows

  [鹤] ni hc | 1.你 2.尼 3.泥 4.拟 >

  letters   build the code (xiaohe shuangpin: "nihc" = 你好)
  1 - 9     pick candidate 1..9 of the current page
  space     pick candidate 1
  .  or =   next page        ,  or -   previous page
  backspace delete a letter
  enter     commit the raw letters
  Esc/Back  cancel the composition
  other key picks candidate 1, then acts normally

Candidates are read from $HOME/term49_xiaohe.dict
(fallback: app/native/xiaohe.dict,
/accounts/1000/shared/misc/term49_xiaohe.dict).

两种输入方式，同一份词典：默认小鹤双拼（固定两键，
如 nihc = 你好），也可以切成全拼（nihao = 你好）。
切换：Menu 键 -> f（标签显示 全/鹤）。固定用全拼就在
~/.term49rc 里写 pinyin_mode = "full";

Backspace sends DEL (0x7f) and the tty erase char is
set to match, so claude/vim/less delete properly. If an
old setup needs ^H instead, put backspace = "bs"; in
~/.term49rc (default is "del").

Menu key (BlackBerry key) opens the action popup: "CN"
toggles the bar, "Kbd" the virtual keyboard, "Paste"
pastes, "Cls" clears, "Fix" rescreens. Start with
TERM49_PINYIN=0 to have the bar off; TERM49_MENU_ALT=1
restores the old Menu = sticky-Alt behaviour.

4. CLASSIC HARDWARE (trackpad, belt keys)

TRACKPAD
  Swipe: arrow keys (history, cursor, vi/less, etc.).
  DECCKM application cursor mode is respected.
  Click: Enter by default (term info "kent").

  Settings in ~/.term49rc:
    trackpad_enabled = true;
    trackpad_sensitivity = 60;   /* lower = more keys/swipe */
    trackpad_click_keys = "kent"; /* "" disables click */

BELT KEYS
  Back: sends Esc (configurable: back_button_keys).
  Menu (BlackBerry key): toggles *persistent* Alt mode —
    every key uses the alt table; Alt+trackpad scroll is
    armed. Indicator 'a' shows while active. Hardware Alt
    remains one-shot. Swipe-down-from-bezel does the same.
  Send / End: keep system behaviour.

===========================================================

5. MOUSE, SCROLLBACK, COPY/PASTE

MOUSE (xterm reporting: DECSET 9/1000/1002/1003, SGR 1006)
When an app enables mouse tracking (tmux mouse, vim
set mouse=a, htop, mc, ...):

  - Trackpad drives a mouse pointer (inverse '+'); click
    is left button. When tracking ends, trackpad returns
    to arrow keys automatically.
  - Touch a cell to click; drag for motion (e.g. select
    in vim).
  - Alt + trackpad: wheel events (app scroll) instead of
    local scrollback.

SCROLLBACK (scrollback_lines, default 500)
  - Touch-drag up/down on the screen
  - Alt + trackpad swipe up/down
  Inverse '▲' top-right while scrolled back. Typing or
  trackpad arrows snaps to the live screen. Alternate-
  screen apps (less, vim) are unaffected.

COPY / PASTE
  - Copy: hold Alt (or Menu's persistent Alt), then drag
    across the screen. Release copies to the system
    clipboard. Tap clears selection.
  - Paste: metamode 'v' (or paste_clipboard). Bracketed
    paste (DECSET 2004) is supported when the app enables
    it.
  - OSC 52: remote programs can set the phone clipboard
    (e.g. tmux set-clipboard). Clipboard *reads* via
    OSC 52 are ignored for privacy.

CJK / DOUBLE-WIDTH
  East-Asian wide characters take two columns. Extra
  system fonts (Hei, MSung, malgun) fill Han/kana/Hangul
  gaps. See font_path, fallback_font_path, cjk_font_path.

===========================================================

6. SHIFT+TAB AND KEY TABLES

With Shift held or sticky shift armed (↑ indicator):
  - Virtual keyboard Tab → back-tab (ESC [ Z)
  - Metamode / sym / alt keys bound to Tab also send
    back-tab
  - Uppercase metamode bindings win when Shift is active
    e.g. ("T", "...") alongside ("t", "\t")

===========================================================

7. PREFERENCES

File: ~/.term49rc (libconfig syntax). Created with
defaults on first launch if missing. Errors fall back
to compiled defaults.

Selected options and Term49X defaults:

font_path = "/usr/fonts/font_repository/dejavu/DejaVuSansMono.ttf";
fallback_font_path = "../app/native/fonts/CascadiaMonoPL-Regular.ttf";
/* Glyphs missing from the main font (box drawing, braille,
 * powerline, ...) use the fallback. Set "" to disable. */

cjk_font_path = "/usr/fonts/font_repository/monotype/MHeiM.GB18030_200910.ttf";
/* Primary CJK face; additional system faces are chained
 * for kana/Hangul. */

font_size = 20;
text_color = [255, 255, 255];
background_color = [0, 0, 0];
screen_idle_awake = false;
auto_show_vkb = true;

metamode_doubletap_key = 61666;     /* KEYCODE_RIGHT_SHIFT — not useful on Classic Q20 */
metamode_doubletap_delay = 500000000;
keyhold_actions = true;
keyhold_actions_exempt = [KEYCODE_BACKSPACE, KEYCODE_RETURN];
metamode_hold_key = 32;             /* Space */
tty_encoding = "UTF-8";
metamode_hitbox : { x = 0; y = 0; w = 100; h = 100; };

metamode_keys : (
    ("e", "\x1B"),
    ("t", "\t")
  );
/* One-shot metamode → string, then exit metamode.
 * terminfo aliases: kcuu1, kcud1, kcuf1, kcub1, khome,
 * kend, kf1..kf12. Hex: \xHH */

metamode_sticky_keys : (
    ("k", "kcuu1"),
    ("j", "kcud1"),
    ("l", "kcuf1"),
    ("h", "kcub1")
  );
/* Like metamode_keys but stay in metamode. */

metamode_func_keys : (
    ("a", "alt_down"),
    ("c", "ctrl_down"),
    ("s", "rescreen"),
    ("v", "paste_clipboard")
  );

main_symmenu : (
  (
    ("q", "\x1b"), ("w", "1"), ("e", "2"), ("r", "3"),
    ("t", "\t"), ("y", "<"), ("u", ">"), ("i", "\x03"),
    ("o", "^"), ("p", "%")
  ),
  (
    ("a", "~"), ("s", "4"), ("d", "5"), ("f", "6"),
    ("g", "\\"), ("h", "|"), ("j", "&"), ("k", "`"),
    ("l", "=")
  ),
  (
    ("0", "0"), ("z", "7"), ("x", "8"), ("c", "9"),
    ("v", "{"), ("b", "}"), ("n", "["), ("m", "]"),
    ("$", "\x1b[A")
  )
);
/* Sym menu: groups are rows bottom→top. */

sticky_sym_key = true;
sticky_shift_key = true;
sticky_alt_key = true;
allow_resize_columns = false;
rescreen_on_symmenu = true;

trackpad_enabled = true;
trackpad_sensitivity = 60;
trackpad_click_keys = "kent";
back_button_keys = "\x1b";
scrollback_lines = 500;

prefs_version = <int>
/* Bumped by the app when defaults change; usually leave alone. */

===========================================================

8. WHAT'S INSTALLED

Stock BB10 userland is sparse. You get basic file tools
(ls, less, mv, …), python, elvis (vi-like). Term49X also
bundles mksh (better Unicode shell), ssh, scp, sftp, and
ssh-keygen under the app private bin path.

===========================================================

9. SOURCE / HELP

Term49X is open source (SDL, freetype, libconfig, ICU,
xterm-style sequences). Project home:

  https://github.com/jxw1102/Term49X

Upstream lineage: BerryFarm/Term49, mordak/Term48.

Bugs, ideas, and pull requests welcome on GitHub.

Happy hacking!
